{
  "step_1": "The decision reason is applicable to the decision problem. The lotteries A and B have the following outcomes: Lottery A: 4000, 2000, 0 (each with probabilities 25%, 25%, 50%); Lottery B: 6000, 0 (with probabilities 25%, 75%). The aspiration level is defined as the mean of all outcomes, ignoring probabilities. The outcomes across both lotteries are: 4000, 2000, 0, 6000, 0. The mean (aspiration level) is (4000 + 2000 + 0 + 6000 + 0) / 5 = 12000 / 5 = 2400 Euros. The differences between each outcome and the aspiration level are: 4000 - 2400 = 1600 (positive), 2000 - 2400 = -400 (negative), 0 - 2400 = -2400 (negative), 6000 - 2400 = 3600 (positive), 0 - 2400 = -2400 (negative). The reason prefers the lottery with the higher probability of positive differences (i.e., outcomes above 2400). In Lottery A: only 4000 > 2400 → 25% probability of positive difference. In Lottery B: only 6000 > 2400 → 25% probability of positive difference. Thus, both lotteries have equal probability (25%) of positive differences. The reason would be indifferent between A and B based on this rule.",
